WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:16.990
<v Speaker 1>Welcome and join us on this deep dive into Reza Shakazemi's spiritual ethos, exploring its subject, the closing portrait of the Imam, peace be upon him, and his spiritual ethos, tracing joy, beauty, detachment, and the hereafter in one seamless arc.

2
00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:18.270
<v Speaker 2>Glad to be here for this one.

3
00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:23.950
<v Speaker 1>The fruit of good action is like its root goodness already tastes the hereafter, here and now.

4
00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:25.230
<v Speaker 2>That is

5
00:00:26.270 --> 00:00:27.870
<v Speaker 2>Such a dense starting point.

6
00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:33.870
<v Speaker 1>I mean, think about the smartest person Just picture the most brilliant, highly educated expert you can think of in your life.

7
00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.230
<v Speaker 2>Okay, I have a few people in mind.

8
00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:36.750
<v Speaker 1>Are they also the happiest person you know?

9
00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:38.990
<v Speaker 2>Oh, definitely not.

10
00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:40.510
<v Speaker 2>Usually they're the most stressed.

11
00:00:40.830 --> 00:00:47.350
<v Speaker 1>We have been conditioned, practically from birth, to believe that acquiring knowledge is like gathering bricks.

12
00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:50.110
<v Speaker 1>It's this heavy, cold, exhausting process.

13
00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:53.630
<v Speaker 1>But today, we are looking at a total inversion of that assumption.

14
00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:58.430
<v Speaker 1>We're exploring why knowledge is actually the most joyful act a human being can undertake.

15
00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:02.590
<v Speaker 2>We must begin by looking at the specific crisis of our current age.

16
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:05.150
<v Speaker 2>You know, how we actually view knowledge today.

17
00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:10.710
<v Speaker 2>Because our civilization has basically trained you to picture knowledge as something fundamentally dry.

18
00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:11.550
<v Speaker 2>It's abstract.

19
00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.910
<v Speaker 2>It's completely separated from your inner life.

20
00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:14.670
<v Speaker 1>It's just data.

21
00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:15.310
<v Speaker 2>It is.

22
00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:22.030
<v Speaker 2>It's treated as a bloodless accumulation of facts or just the mechanical running of procedures.

23
00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:26.430
<v Speaker 2>In this modern framing, you can acquire vast amounts of information.

24
00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:32.110
<v Speaker 2>You can become an undisputed master in your field, and yet your heart remains completely unchanged.

25
00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:33.630
<v Speaker 1>Unmoved by any of it.

26
00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:37.310
<v Speaker 2>Because knowledge is seen merely as a utilitarian instrument.

27
00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:39.710
<v Speaker 2>It's a tool we use to manipulate the physical world.

28
00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:48.110
<v Speaker 2>to build bridges or cure diseases or win arguments online, but it is entirely divorced from the condition of the soul.

29
00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:50.990
<v Speaker 1>That divorce leaves the soul so impoverished.

30
00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:56.350
<v Speaker 1>And you can see it in how exhausted modern people feel, even when they're surrounded by limitless information.

31
00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:59.310
<v Speaker 1>We have all the facts in the world, but very little gladness.

32
00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:06.430
<v Speaker 1>And this is where we encounter the imam's radical claim, which turns our modern assumption completely upside down.

33
00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:12.430
<v Speaker 1>In the collected sermons, he states that the most joyous of mankind is the intellectual.

34
00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:14.630
<v Speaker 2>Just sit with the weight of that inversion for a moment.

35
00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:20.190
<v Speaker 1>He does not say the wisest person is simply most correct or the most accurate or the most well-read.

36
00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:22.750
<v Speaker 1>He says the wisest is the happiest.

37
00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:25.230
<v Speaker 2>That feels so counterintuitive to how we live.

38
00:02:25.230 --> 00:02:25.710
<v Speaker 1>It does.

39
00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:34.190
<v Speaker 1>The brutal, agonizing struggle against the ego, the campaign against the caprice of one's own soul and that heavy pull of appetite,

40
00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.590
<v Speaker 1>This is not waged merely to avoid defeat.

41
00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:38.590
<v Speaker 1>It is waged for joy.

42
00:02:39.230 --> 00:02:44.830
<v Speaker 1>The discipline of the mind and the purification of the heart are directed toward a profound, lasting delight.

43
00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:49.950
<v Speaker 2>We can frame this pursuit of knowledge as a homecoming from the exile of ignorance.

44
00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:52.430
<v Speaker 2>I mean, think about what it actually means to be in exile.

45
00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:53.470
<v Speaker 1>It's a painful state.

46
00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:54.110
<v Speaker 2>It is.

47
00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:58.830
<v Speaker 2>To be ignorant is not simply to have an empty folder on a computer hard drive just, you know, waiting for data.

48
00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:01.950
<v Speaker 2>To be ignorant is to be lost in a hostile wilderness.

49
00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:03.630
<v Speaker 1>That's a powerful way to put it.

50
00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:04.390
<v Speaker 2>Is to be

51
00:03:04.830 --> 00:03:07.550
<v Speaker 2>from your own true nature and from reality itself.

52
00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:10.590
<v Speaker 2>It's a state of cold, disoriented wandering.

53
00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:14.830
<v Speaker 2>When you do not know who you are or what the world is, you suffer.

54
00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:16.110
<v Speaker 1>You're just stumbling in the dark.

55
00:03:16.190 --> 00:03:20.190
<v Speaker 2>Therefore, to know in this deep spiritual sense is to find the way back.

56
00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:24.590
<v Speaker 2>It's the joy of arriving at the hearth after a long, freezing banishment.

57
00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:28.430
<v Speaker 2>The warmth of the fire is the truth you finally comprehend.

58
00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:31.230
<v Speaker 1>Finding the way back requires a map, though.

59
00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:35.630
<v Speaker 1>And for the Imam, that map begins with the priority of worship.

60
00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:36.430
<v Speaker 1>It's

61
00:03:36.470 --> 00:03:42.190
<v Speaker 1>It's crucial to examine where worship sits in his order of things because, well, it is not where modern people usually put it.

62
00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:43.390
<v Speaker 2>Where do we usually put it?

63
00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:45.390
<v Speaker 2>At the end of the day, if there's time left over.

64
00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:46.990
<v Speaker 1>If we put it anywhere at all, yes.

65
00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:53.950
<v Speaker 1>But we find a profound doorway in the Quranic verse, chapter 6, verse 162, which provides the foundational sequence.

66
00:03:53.950 --> 00:04:00.830
<v Speaker 1>It says, say, truly my worship and my sacrifice and my living and my dying are for God, Lord of the universes.

67
00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:02.990
<v Speaker 2>Notice the specific order of the words there.

68
00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.390
<v Speaker 1>Worship is placed before living, before dying, and before death.

69
00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:07.310
<v Speaker 1>or sacrifice.

70
00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:09.310
<v Speaker 1>Worship comes first.

71
00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:12.990
<v Speaker 1>It is not an afterthought and it is not merely a formal obligation.

72
00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:14.910
<v Speaker 1>You just add on to an already busy schedule.

73
00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:20.710
<v Speaker 2>Worship is the central pillar because it sustains a dimension of the soul that simply withers without it.

74
00:04:20.710 --> 00:04:21.390
<v Speaker 1>Starves.

75
00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:25.950
<v Speaker 2>There is a part of the human being that yearns to turn toward its creator.

76
00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:31.310
<v Speaker 2>If you deny that turning, if you pretend that dimension does not exist, that part of you just.

77
00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:38.590
<v Speaker 1>The deepest reason to give this spiritual dimension its due is not out of a begrudging sense of bureaucratic duty.

78
00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:41.310
<v Speaker 1>You do not worship simply because you are told to.

79
00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:45.310
<v Speaker 1>You worship because of the immense reward that lies on the far side of it.

80
00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:46.510
<v Speaker 2>And what is that reward?

81
00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:53.230
<v Speaker 1>It is what the Imam describes in his counsel to the disciple he counsels as intimacy with the spirit of certainty.

82
00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:57.470
<v Speaker 1>Those who have received the life of the spirit rejoice in this intimacy.

83
00:04:57.470 --> 00:04:58.510
<v Speaker 2>I don't buy that yet.

84
00:04:58.510 --> 00:05:01.710
<v Speaker 2>How can knowledge be joy when it feels so dry and cold?

85
00:05:01.950 --> 00:05:04.230
<v Speaker 2>Intellectual work doesn't feel like a physical embrace.

86
00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:05.230
<v Speaker 1>That's a fair challenge.

87
00:05:05.470 --> 00:05:13.230
<v Speaker 2>When you are sifting through difficult philosophical concepts or trying to discipline your mind, it often feels alienating.

88
00:05:13.710 --> 00:05:14.750
<v Speaker 2>and exhausting.

89
00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:17.950
<v Speaker 2>It feels like strenuous effort, not an intimate reunion.

90
00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:19.710
<v Speaker 1>So how do we bridge the gap?

91
00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:25.870
<v Speaker 2>How do we bridge the gap between the grueling work of the intellect and this overwhelming intimate joy you are describing?

92
00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:32.910
<v Speaker 1>I hear that pushback, and it is crucial to address it by unpacking the paradox of what fundamental truths actually are.

93
00:05:33.310 --> 00:05:34.870
<v Speaker 1>We have to break down the language here for a second.

94
00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:35.870
<v Speaker 2>Okay, let's break it down.

95
00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:40.270
<v Speaker 1>The concept of intimacy we are discussing is rooted in a specific Arabic term, pashara,

96
00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:43.550
<v Speaker 1>which translates to a state of direct physical contact.

97
00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:45.470
<v Speaker 2>Like actually touching something.

98
00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:48.270
<v Speaker 1>It does not mean arriving at a cold logical conclusion.

99
00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:53.230
<v Speaker 1>It doesn't mean looking at a mathematical proof, verifying the equations and just nodding your head.

100
00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.750
<v Speaker 1>It means to touch and to be touched by the real.

101
00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:58.990
<v Speaker 2>That's a very different experience of knowing.

102
00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:02.430
<v Speaker 1>Engaging with fundamental truths is not about memorizing data.

103
00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:04.830
<v Speaker 1>It is a return to being itself.

104
00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.990
<v Speaker 1>Reality, the sheer fact of existence, cannot be divided.

105
00:06:09.390 --> 00:06:12.750
<v Speaker 1>There is no second reality alongside the absolute.

106
00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:14.830
<v Speaker 2>Because the absolute is all there is.

107
00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:22.030
<v Speaker 1>Therefore, the being of a single human person cannot, at its deepest foundation, be something other than being itself.

108
00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:27.710
<v Speaker 1>Because reality is undivided, knowing reality is the ultimate form of self-discovery.

109
00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:35.310
<v Speaker 1>And coming to know what truly is, you are coming home to what you most deeply are, the grueling effort you mentioned earlier.

110
00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:37.550
<v Speaker 1>That is simply the friction of the ego.

111
00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:39.230
<v Speaker 1>resisting this reality.

112
00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:41.230
<v Speaker 2>That homecoming requires a toll then.

113
00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:41.950
<v Speaker 1>A heavy toll.

114
00:06:42.270 --> 00:06:46.190
<v Speaker 1>It demands the deepest possible effacement of the small I.

115
00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:46.830
<v Speaker 1>The ego.

116
00:06:46.910 --> 00:06:56.110
<v Speaker 1>The fragile, separate ego that constantly seeks its own validation, that feels exhausted by study, that feels threatened by what it does not know, that ego must be let go.

117
00:06:56.510 --> 00:07:00.910
<v Speaker 1>The joy we are speaking of belongs to the core of existence, not to the ego.

118
00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:03.830
<v Speaker 2>Think about how unstable ego-driven pleasure actually is.

119
00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:05.070
<v Speaker 1>It's incredibly fragile.

120
00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:08.910
<v Speaker 2>A pleasure that belongs to the ego rises and falls with the ego's fortunes.

121
00:07:09.310 --> 00:07:10.910
<v Speaker 2>If you receive praise, you are happy.

122
00:07:11.150 --> 00:07:14.910
<v Speaker 2>If someone criticizes you an hour later, you are completely devastated.

123
00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:24.670
<v Speaker 1>But the joy of certainty is unshakable because it is the gladness of being itself, tasted most fully in the very act of the ego's surrender.

124
00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:32.270
<v Speaker 1>The divine beatitude, the sheer contentment of the soul, is described as greater than the garden of paradise.

125
00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:34.590
<v Speaker 2>Wait, greater than paradise itself?

126
00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:38.670
<v Speaker 1>The joy of this touch is greater than the ultimate destination of heaven itself.

127
00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:42.670
<v Speaker 1>It depends entirely on your soul being true to the spirit within it.

128
00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:46.990
<v Speaker 2>This brings us to a specific type of person, the one who embodies the certainty.

129
00:07:47.630 --> 00:07:52.110
<v Speaker 2>The texts redefine a common term, which we can translate conceptually as

130
00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:54.190
<v Speaker 2>The one who truly understands.

131
00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:56.910
<v Speaker 1>Let's unpack that because it's so easily misunderstood.

132
00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:04.430
<v Speaker 2>In a conventional sense, people often assume that the one who truly understands religion or philosophy is, well, the most severe person in the room.

133
00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:05.390
<v Speaker 1>The strictest one.

134
00:08:05.470 --> 00:08:13.430
<v Speaker 2>We picture the strictest judge, the one who is quickest to point out flaws, the one who delivers the harshest rulings and sets the most rigid boundaries.

135
00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:15.870
<v Speaker 1>That's usually imamate we have of a religious scholar.

136
00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.830
<v Speaker 2>But the imam completely overturns this assumption.

137
00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:21.910
<v Speaker 2>He says the one who truly understands is recognized

138
00:08:22.070 --> 00:08:23.230
<v Speaker 2>their merciful bearing.

139
00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:26.190
<v Speaker 1>They never make a soul despair of God's mercy.

140
00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:30.110
<v Speaker 1>And we need to pause here, because despair is not just a sad feeling.

141
00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:34.510
<v Speaker 1>In this framework, despair is an error in knowledge.

142
00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:36.030
<v Speaker 2>An epistemic error.

143
00:08:36.270 --> 00:08:40.990
<v Speaker 1>To give up on mercy is a failure to know the divine as it has revealed itself.

144
00:08:41.710 --> 00:08:44.350
<v Speaker 1>The divine has prescribed mercy for himself.

145
00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:46.510
<v Speaker 1>Consider the gravity of that statement.

146
00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:49.390
<v Speaker 1>The all-powerful has bound himself to compassion.

147
00:08:49.390 --> 00:08:54.270
<v Speaker 2>So if you despair, you are fundamentally misunderstanding the nature of reality.

148
00:08:54.270 --> 00:09:02.590
<v Speaker 1>For a person to truly understand this, they must be so immersed in the joyous certainty of that mercy that despair simply cannot take root in them.

149
00:09:03.230 --> 00:09:06.750
<v Speaker 1>Their certainty in this boundless mercy brings an unshakable joy.

150
00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:08.590
<v Speaker 2>And that joy radiates outward.

151
00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:11.950
<v Speaker 1>It radiates outward as a merciful bearing toward everyone they encounter.

152
00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:14.750
<v Speaker 1>They do not act as gatekeepers to a closed fortress.

153
00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:16.830
<v Speaker 1>They act as guides to an open sanctuary.

154
00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:22.430
<v Speaker 2>If you have this joyous certainty, this merciful bearing, it changes how you interact with the physical world around you too.

155
00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:23.470
<v Speaker 1>It changes everything.

156
00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:27.150
<v Speaker 2>We encounter another unexpected element in this spiritual landscape.

157
00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:35.550
<v Speaker 2>This opening onto joy through intimate knowledge turns out to be inseparable from something our modern age rarely associates with rigorous intellect.

158
00:09:35.630 --> 00:09:36.190
<v Speaker 1>Beauty.

159
00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:39.790
<v Speaker 2>We have been conditioned to think of beauty as something purely decorative.

160
00:09:40.030 --> 00:09:46.830
<v Speaker 2>We treat it as a subjective flourish, you know, a matter of personal taste that you just add on after the real work of thinking is done.

161
00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:49.310
<v Speaker 1>Like putting a nice coat of paint on a house after it's built.

162
00:09:49.470 --> 00:09:52.270
<v Speaker 2>But in this ethos, beauty is not a decoration.

163
00:09:52.350 --> 00:09:56.590
<v Speaker 1>Appreciating the beautiful is what a rightly working intelligence is actually for.

164
00:09:57.150 --> 00:09:57.590
<v Speaker 1>It is an

165
00:09:57.670 --> 00:09:59.390
<v Speaker 1>intrinsic function of the intellect.

166
00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:02.910
<v Speaker 1>The connection here leads us straight to the heart of virtue.

167
00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:06.670
<v Speaker 2>The literal meaning of virtue in this context is making beautiful.

168
00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:08.670
<v Speaker 1>Making beautiful, let that sink in.

169
00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:16.110
<v Speaker 2>The excellence of the intellect is found in the beauty of things outward, but it is completed and anchored by a beauty of soul within.

170
00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:21.150
<v Speaker 2>Virtue is the active, deliberate process of making the soul beautiful.

171
00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:23.630
<v Speaker 2>It is not just about avoiding bad deeds.

172
00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:26.510
<v Speaker 2>It is an aesthetic triumph of the inner life.

173
00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:29.470
<v Speaker 1>Let us examine our second governing image.

174
00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:34.590
<v Speaker 1>The soul made beautiful, drawing down the love of the beautiful.

175
00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:41.230
<v Speaker 1>This image rests on 2 essential sayings from the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family.

176
00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:42.510
<v Speaker 2>What are those sayings?

177
00:10:43.070 --> 00:10:45.710
<v Speaker 1>The first is that God is beautiful and loves beauty.

178
00:10:46.350 --> 00:10:49.310
<v Speaker 1>The second is the command to assume the character traits of God.

179
00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:55.230
<v Speaker 1>When you lay these two together, a complete, seamless spiritual program emerges.

180
00:10:55.550 --> 00:11:05.790
<v Speaker 2>If the divine is beautiful and loves beauty, and if your calling as a human being is to assume those divine character traits, then virtue is far more profound than following a set of external rules.

181
00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:07.790
<v Speaker 1>It's not just a checklist of do's and don'ts.

182
00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:11.470
<v Speaker 2>Virtue is the active work of making yourself lovable to the divine.

183
00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:16.190
<v Speaker 2>It is the cultivation of inner beauty so that you might draw down the love of the one who loves the beautiful.

184
00:11:17.150 --> 00:11:22.750
<v Speaker 1>It is an upward reach of human love met by a downward reach of divine love, and the meeting place is the beautiful soul.

185
00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:31.550
<v Speaker 2>The virtues required for this, kindness, contentment, courtesy, generosity and modesty, and a wise, unhurried forbearance are what give the intellect depth.

186
00:11:31.790 --> 00:11:33.790
<v Speaker 1>Forbearance is such a key word there.

187
00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:38.910
<v Speaker 1>It's the strength to hold your temper and absorb a wrong without being diminished by it.

188
00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:44.950
<v Speaker 1>Without these traits, the mind remains trapped on a flat, horizontal plane of mere rationality.

189
00:11:46.310 --> 00:11:48.630
<v Speaker 1>Virtue aerates the intelligence.

190
00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:52.150
<v Speaker 2>The Aga Khan has expanded beautifully on this principle.

191
00:11:52.390 --> 00:12:02.870
<v Speaker 2>He has argued that beauty in the made world, whether it is the perfect tadence of a line of verse, the harmonious proportions of a building, or the meticulous care put into a garden, is not a luxury.

192
00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:04.390
<v Speaker 1>It's not just extra fluff.

193
00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:06.870
<v Speaker 2>This crafted beauty ennobles life.

194
00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:11.590
<v Speaker 2>It serves as a physical reminder that turns the soul back toward its source.

195
00:12:12.150 --> 00:12:16.390
<v Speaker 2>Laboring for the beautiful in the physical world is a mode of searching for the divine.

196
00:12:16.390 --> 00:12:23.590
<v Speaker 1>The work of making beautiful runs continuously from the inner state of a single individual's heart to the outward architecture of a whole community.

197
00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:29.990
<v Speaker 2>But we must look at what happens when this process is corrupted, because a portrait of virtue inevitably casts a shadow.

198
00:12:30.390 --> 00:12:32.390
<v Speaker 1>Vices are not just moral failings.

199
00:12:32.470 --> 00:12:33.430
<v Speaker 1>They are blind spots.

200
00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:36.790
<v Speaker 1>They are diseases of the heart that destroy the intellect's power to see.

201
00:12:37.110 --> 00:12:40.630
<v Speaker 1>And the subtlest, most insidious darkening of the heart is pretension.

202
00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:41.590
<v Speaker 2>Pretension

203
00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:43.350
<v Speaker 2>Performing for the crowd.

204
00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:46.430
<v Speaker 1>Pretension is the performance of piety for the watching crowd.

205
00:12:46.870 --> 00:12:51.670
<v Speaker 1>It is doing something outwardly good, but doing it entirely to be seen by others.

206
00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:56.150
<v Speaker 2>Pretension is devastating because it wears the exact mask of devotion.

207
00:12:56.310 --> 00:12:57.990
<v Speaker 2>It looked like virtue from the outside.

208
00:12:57.990 --> 00:12:59.910
<v Speaker 1>You can't tell the difference just by looking.

209
00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:07.670
<v Speaker 2>You might see someone giving generously in the public square or leading the community in long prayers, but the diagnosis here is unsparing.

210
00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:10.470
<v Speaker 2>Pretension is the subtlest idol.

211
00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:11.590
<v Speaker 1>The subtlest idol.

212
00:13:11.830 --> 00:13:13.350
<v Speaker 1>That is a terrifying phrase.

213
00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:15.750
<v Speaker 2>It is the setting up of partners beside the divine.

214
00:13:15.990 --> 00:13:24.710
<v Speaker 2>When you perform a good deed, not out of a pure inner alignment, but simply to be praised by the people around you, have made an idol of your audience.

215
00:13:24.710 --> 00:13:27.190
<v Speaker 1>You have placed their gaze above the gaze of the real.

216
00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:29.190
<v Speaker 1>You are worshipping their approval.

217
00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:33.990
<v Speaker 2>This leads to a severe yet ultimately freeing rule regarding human action.

218
00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:37.430
<v Speaker 2>Action is dust, except what is purified within it.

219
00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:40.550
<v Speaker 1>You can perform 1000 outwardly impressive deeds.

220
00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:43.030
<v Speaker 1>You can build monuments of charity.

221
00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:45.510
<v Speaker 1>You can exhaust yourself in public service.

222
00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:49.750
<v Speaker 1>You can be celebrated by your entire community as a pillar of righteousness.

223
00:13:50.070 --> 00:13:58.150
<v Speaker 2>But if the intention within those actions is corrupted by the desire for public acclaim, the actions themselves are merely dust blowing in the wind.

224
00:13:58.470 --> 00:14:01.670
<v Speaker 1>Only the sincerity inside the deed survives the sifting.

225
00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:06.470
<v Speaker 1>Only the pure, beautiful intention has weight in the scales of eternity.

226
00:14:06.630 --> 00:14:12.470
<v Speaker 2>The fruit of good action is like its root goodness already tastes the hereafter here and now.

227
00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:15.190
<v Speaker 1>This brings us to a crucial pivot in our examination.

228
00:14:15.590 --> 00:14:21.030
<v Speaker 1>We must consider the demanding counsel provided regarding how the wise interact with the difficulties of the world.

229
00:14:21.110 --> 00:14:23.910
<v Speaker 2>Because it's not all just joy and beauty in a vacuum.

230
00:14:23.910 --> 00:14:24.710
<v Speaker 2>There's hardship.

231
00:14:24.790 --> 00:14:29.110
<v Speaker 1>The texts state that the wise make easy what the extravagant find harsh.

232
00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:31.750
<v Speaker 1>They befriend what estranges the ignorant.

233
00:14:32.230 --> 00:14:34.790
<v Speaker 1>They do not merely endure hardship with gritted teeth.

234
00:14:34.950 --> 00:14:36.270
<v Speaker 1>They actively befriend it.

235
00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:37.270
<v Speaker 2>I have to stop you there.

236
00:14:37.270 --> 00:14:39.830
<v Speaker 2>Isn't this just a sour hatred of the physical world?

237
00:14:40.310 --> 00:14:42.470
<v Speaker 2>The imam calls the world a chewed up bone.

238
00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:44.950
<v Speaker 2>How does that reconcile with loving its beauty?

239
00:14:45.110 --> 00:14:46.790
<v Speaker 1>It does sound contradictory at first glance.

240
00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:50.550
<v Speaker 2>It sounds like a grim, ascetic rejection of everything physical.

241
00:14:51.030 --> 00:15:01.110
<v Speaker 2>If we are supposed to appreciate the beauty of the made world, as we just discussed, and see how it turns the soul back to its source, how can we simultaneously view the world as contemptible?

242
00:15:01.270 --> 00:15:05.670
<v Speaker 2>How is this not just an ascetic, miserable hatred of physical life?

243
00:15:06.110 --> 00:15:10.070
<v Speaker 1>This tension is vital to navigate and requires careful distinction.

244
00:15:10.790 --> 00:15:15.190
<v Speaker 1>Detachment does not mean a sour war against the body or a hatred of the physical realm.

245
00:15:15.830 --> 00:15:19.270
<v Speaker 1>Detachment overcomes worldliness, not the world itself.

246
00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:22.190
<v Speaker 2>Okay, unpack the difference between the world and worldliness.

247
00:15:22.230 --> 00:15:25.350
<v Speaker 1>Worldliness is the egoistic clinging to passing things.

248
00:15:25.750 --> 00:15:31.590
<v Speaker 1>It is treating temporary conditions, wealth, status, physical comfort as if they were ultimate realities.

249
00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:35.750
<v Speaker 1>The world is only small when it's compared directly to the absolute.

250
00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:45.590
<v Speaker 1>The imam who calls the world a chewed up bone is the very same imam who speaks with unmistakable wonder about the beauty of the peacock and the marvels of virgin nature.

251
00:15:45.590 --> 00:15:46.950
<v Speaker 2>So it's about proportion.

252
00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:52.470
<v Speaker 1>The world is contemptible only when it is loved for itself, as an idol, as a final destination.

253
00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:55.430
<v Speaker 1>But it is glorious when it is seen as a hymn.

254
00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:57.670
<v Speaker 1>The world still hymns God's praise.

255
00:15:58.070 --> 00:16:01.510
<v Speaker 1>Every created thing points beyond itself to the Creator.

256
00:16:01.670 --> 00:16:04.070
<v Speaker 2>We can understand this through the story of Joseph.

257
00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:10.070
<v Speaker 2>In the scriptures, Joseph's brothers deemed him of little value and sold him for a paltry price.

258
00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:15.150
<v Speaker 1>The word used to describe their valuation of him is the root of the word for detachment.

259
00:16:15.350 --> 00:16:19.510
<v Speaker 2>Detachment is placing little value on the world when it is weighed against eternity.

260
00:16:19.750 --> 00:16:24.630
<v Speaker 2>It is not that the world has zero value, but its value is instrumental, not ultimate.

261
00:16:25.150 --> 00:16:31.190
<v Speaker 1>When you detach from the frantic pursuit of worldly accumulation, you experience a strange enlargement of the soul.

262
00:16:31.350 --> 00:16:37.750
<v Speaker 2>Think about how much energy is consumed by anxiety over worldly status, just constantly worrying about how you are perceived.

263
00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:41.910
<v Speaker 1>The less your soul is crowded by that anxiety, the more room it has for the real.

264
00:16:42.390 --> 00:16:44.550
<v Speaker 1>Detachment does not subtract from your life.

265
00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:47.750
<v Speaker 1>It expands your capacity to experience true existence.

266
00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:52.710
<v Speaker 2>This expansion leads us to the ultimate detachment, which is the contemplation of death.

267
00:16:53.270 --> 00:16:58.710
<v Speaker 2>We introduce our third governing image here, death as something deeply familiar and nurturing.

268
00:16:58.950 --> 00:17:03.190
<v Speaker 1>The imam says death is more intimate than the mother's breast is to the suckling babe.

269
00:17:03.670 --> 00:17:10.710
<v Speaker 2>He is speaking of death not as something to be braved or stoically endured, but as the nearest and most nourishing of intimacies.

270
00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:12.790
<v Speaker 1>Consider the state of the suckling babe.

271
00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:17.590
<v Speaker 1>It is fragile, entirely dependent, seeking warmth and sustenance.

272
00:17:18.230 --> 00:17:23.830
<v Speaker 1>The mother's breast represents total integration, safety, and the quenching of a fundamental thirst.

273
00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:25.190
<v Speaker 2>That sounds like wordplay.

274
00:17:25.590 --> 00:17:27.990
<v Speaker 2>Isn't loving death just dressing up a fear of it?

275
00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:29.190
<v Speaker 1>It can sound like that, sure.

276
00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:31.350
<v Speaker 2>It feels like a psychological defense mechanism.

277
00:17:31.510 --> 00:17:41.110
<v Speaker 2>To call death a mother's breast sounds like a poetic way to avoid confronting the terrifying reality of non-existence and the violent severance from everything we know and love in this life.

278
00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:45.110
<v Speaker 2>How can the end of life be framed as a nurturing intimacy?

279
00:17:45.350 --> 00:17:49.270
<v Speaker 1>Let us look closer at the mechanism of dying to self while still alive.

280
00:17:49.750 --> 00:17:53.190
<v Speaker 1>The texts are pointing toward an extinction of the ego before the absolute.

281
00:17:53.510 --> 00:17:56.150
<v Speaker 2>Meaning you don't wait for physical death.

282
00:17:56.470 --> 00:18:01.430
<v Speaker 1>Life, when lived purely within the confines of the ego, is actually a walled-in privation.

283
00:18:02.150 --> 00:18:07.510
<v Speaker 1>You are trapped in a tiny fortress of your own anxieties, your desires, your need to defend your identity.

284
00:18:07.510 --> 00:18:09.830
<v Speaker 2>It's exhausting just holding up those walls.

285
00:18:09.910 --> 00:18:12.630
<v Speaker 1>To extinguish that ego is not to cease existing.

286
00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:16.230
<v Speaker 1>It is to plunge into the infinite plenitude of the divine realms.

287
00:18:17.030 --> 00:18:19.910
<v Speaker 1>Death for the wise is a release.

288
00:18:20.470 --> 00:18:21.750
<v Speaker 1>It is an integration.

289
00:18:21.910 --> 00:18:26.470
<v Speaker 1>It is a profound mode of tawhid, of ultimate unification with the one.

290
00:18:26.550 --> 00:18:27.190
<v Speaker 2>Oh, I see.

291
00:18:27.430 --> 00:18:32.630
<v Speaker 1>The ignorant recoil from death because they think their small walled in ego is their true and only life.

292
00:18:33.030 --> 00:18:34.550
<v Speaker 1>They confuse the prison for the self.

293
00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:39.670
<v Speaker 1>But the surrender of that small self is the only ground upon which infinite life can be experienced.

294
00:18:39.670 --> 00:18:40.710
<v Speaker 2>Okay, I see it now.

295
00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:44.630
<v Speaker 2>When you frame it as dying to the small self, the resistance begins to dissolve.

296
00:18:45.190 --> 00:18:51.550
<v Speaker 2>If the ego is a clenched fist, opening the hand feels like death to the fist, but it is the only way to hold anything else.

297
00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:53.030
<v Speaker 1>That's a beautiful way to see it.

298
00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:57.430
<v Speaker 2>I can see how this perspective was embraced by those who walked this path deeply.

299
00:18:57.990 --> 00:19:07.110
<v Speaker 2>The early Sufi master understood that to die to the small self is not to be extinguished, but to be raised into a higher unobstructed life.

300
00:19:07.270 --> 00:19:15.510
<v Speaker 1>And the Persian poet articulated this beautifully when he asked his companions not to weep at his funeral, because for him, death was not an ending, but a wedding.

301
00:19:15.830 --> 00:19:18.070
<v Speaker 2>A wedding, the ultimate union.

302
00:19:18.070 --> 00:19:22.630
<v Speaker 1>It is the consummation toward which the entire life of the spirit has been quietly moving.

303
00:19:23.590 --> 00:19:31.110
<v Speaker 1>The intimacy of the mother's breast makes perfect sense if the soul has already practiced surrendering its boundaries through love and knowledge.

304
00:19:31.190 --> 00:19:41.350
<v Speaker 2>To ground these incredibly high concepts, we must look at a historical example of what happens when the outward form of devotion is completely severed from this inner dying to self.

305
00:19:41.830 --> 00:19:44.470
<v Speaker 2>Let us examine the historical pious rebels.

306
00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:47.990
<v Speaker 1>We need to steel man their devotion to understand the contrast clearly.

307
00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:49.270
<v Speaker 1>Let's give them their due.

308
00:19:49.510 --> 00:19:52.710
<v Speaker 2>Picture them at their absolute strongest, their most about.

309
00:19:53.030 --> 00:19:53.310
<v Speaker 2>These

310
00:19:53.430 --> 00:19:56.870
<v Speaker 2>individuals celebrated throughout their communities for their scriptural reading.

311
00:19:57.030 --> 00:20:00.070
<v Speaker 2>They performed punishing night-long vigils of worship.

312
00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:01.990
<v Speaker 1>They fasted relentlessly.

313
00:20:01.990 --> 00:20:07.910
<v Speaker 2>Their outward zeal was unmatched, and they were admired by many for the sheer physical stamina of their piety.

314
00:20:08.710 --> 00:20:12.790
<v Speaker 2>They possessed A discipline that most people could not even begin to emulate.

315
00:20:13.110 --> 00:20:17.030
<v Speaker 1>And yet they lacked the fundamental core of what the Imam teaches.

316
00:20:17.590 --> 00:20:22.550
<v Speaker 1>They lacked the inner beauty, the merciful bearing, the true understanding that prevents despair.

317
00:20:22.790 --> 00:20:24.550
<v Speaker 1>Their zeal had turned bitter.

318
00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:26.470
<v Speaker 2>It was a caricature of true devotion.

319
00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:29.030
<v Speaker 2>They were severe, harsh, and quick to judge.

320
00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:36.150
<v Speaker 1>And so the imam delivered a startling rebuke to his own supporters, who were expressing admiration for these rebels.

321
00:20:36.390 --> 00:20:40.310
<v Speaker 1>He told them, sleeping with certainty is better than praying with doubt.

322
00:20:40.630 --> 00:20:42.390
<v Speaker 2>Think about the magnitude of that statement.

323
00:20:43.030 --> 00:20:47.830
<v Speaker 2>Picture the rebel who hasn't slept in three days, who is physically exhausted from continuous prayer.

324
00:20:48.230 --> 00:20:53.270
<v Speaker 2>The imam basically looks at him and says, you would be better off taking a nap if you actually knew what you were doing.

325
00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:56.070
<v Speaker 1>The mechanism behind this rebuke is profound.

326
00:20:56.710 --> 00:21:00.230
<v Speaker 1>Worship without the seeing heart is nothing more than mechanical motion.

327
00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:01.430
<v Speaker 2>It's just gymnastics.

328
00:21:01.590 --> 00:21:11.750
<v Speaker 1>The bodily rest of a soul that is inwardly awake to higher realities, a soul resting in the joyous certainty of divine mercy, takes absolutely nothing away from its true wakefulness.

329
00:21:11.830 --> 00:21:16.310
<v Speaker 2>The pious rebels were physically awake all night, but spiritually they were completely blind.

330
00:21:16.390 --> 00:21:18.150
<v Speaker 1>True intellect is the seeing heart.

331
00:21:19.030 --> 00:21:28.790
<v Speaker 1>If you do not have that seeing heart, if you do not possess the inner virtue of making beautiful, then the most strenuous physical devotion is entirely disconnected from the divine.

332
00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:34.070
<v Speaker 2>It becomes an exercise in ego, a form of spiritual pride masquerading as submission.

333
00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:39.430
<v Speaker 1>The obedience to the command to pray is necessary, but it is not sufficient on its own.

334
00:21:40.070 --> 00:21:43.270
<v Speaker 1>There must be a profound consciousness of what you are doing and why.

335
00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:49.430
<v Speaker 1>This awareness joins seamlessly to inward beauty that actually draws down divine acceptance.

336
00:21:49.430 --> 00:21:55.190
<v Speaker 2>The hereafter is not a distant future payment that you were waiting for after a long, miserable life of deprivation.

337
00:21:55.350 --> 00:21:57.590
<v Speaker 2>The hereafter is already beneath our feet.

338
00:21:57.750 --> 00:22:03.830
<v Speaker 1>The imam counsels us to cast the eye of the heart toward the garden, to trees whose roots lie hidden in dunes of musk.

339
00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:07.270
<v Speaker 1>This is not a rhetorical flourish meant to bribe you into good behavior.

340
00:22:07.430 --> 00:22:09.030
<v Speaker 2>It's a description of a vision.

341
00:22:09.110 --> 00:22:10.150
<v Speaker 1>It is a foretaste.

342
00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:14.630
<v Speaker 1>that is actually available right now to the soul that has grown still uncertain.

343
00:22:15.350 --> 00:22:19.030
<v Speaker 1>It changes the entire landscape of how you live your life today.

344
00:22:19.190 --> 00:22:21.510
<v Speaker 2>The texts ask a penetrating question.

345
00:22:21.990 --> 00:22:25.110
<v Speaker 2>Is the reward of goodness ought but goodness?

346
00:22:25.270 --> 00:22:28.470
<v Speaker 1>Goodness and its reward are made of the exact same substance.

347
00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:33.990
<v Speaker 2>You do not endure the bitter medicine of goodness now to get a sweet candy of reward later.

348
00:22:34.550 --> 00:22:37.590
<v Speaker 2>The goodness itself contains the sweetness.

349
00:22:37.910 --> 00:22:43.670
<v Speaker 1>On the classical Ismaili reading, salvation is not merely a verdict delivered at the end of time by a distant judge.

350
00:22:44.230 --> 00:22:49.670
<v Speaker 1>Salvation is a deliverance that begins right now, today, grounded in unshakable certitude.

351
00:22:49.910 --> 00:22:57.110
<v Speaker 2>Heaven is being tasted in every act of pure intention, in every cultivation of inner beauty, and in every moment of true intellectual joy.

352
00:22:57.270 --> 00:23:02.470
<v Speaker 1>The fruit of good action is like its goodness only tastes the hereafter, here and now.

353
00:23:02.950 --> 00:23:10.390
<v Speaker 1>The spiritual ethos we have explored reveals a life where knowing, loving, and living are fused into a single act and that act is joy.

354
00:23:10.710 --> 00:23:15.750
<v Speaker 2>The wisest person is the happiest, not despite their rigorous discipline, but entirely because of it.

355
00:23:15.910 --> 00:23:18.550
<v Speaker 1>The most detached person is the most fully alive.

356
00:23:19.430 --> 00:23:24.470
<v Speaker 1>The one who is most intimately acquainted with the death of the ego is the one who has truly begun to live.

357
00:23:25.310 --> 00:23:27.990
<v Speaker 1>Which good do you already know you've refused to begin?
